ized on the privilege alone\",\n+\t\t\t\t    type != null ? type.getName() : null, id.getClass().getName());\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\treturn true;\n+\t\t}\n+\n+\t\tfor (DomainObjectAuthorizationRule rule : applicable) {\n+\t\t\tif (!rule.isAuthorized(authentication, serializableId, type, permission)) {\n+\t\t\t\tlogDenial(rule, type, permission, serializableId);\n+\t\t\t\treturn false;\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\treturn true;\n+\t}\n+\n+\t/**\n+\t * Records which rule refused, at debug because a rule denying is this extension point working\n+\t * rather than anything wrong. The denial the caller sees names the expression but never the rule:\n+\t * which classes a deployment runs is not something to put in a response body.\n+\t * <p>\n+\t * The target itself is deliberately not logged - only its type, the privilege and its identifier:\n+\t * the id an object form can give and its uuid otherwise (see {@link #identifierOf(Object)}), the id\n+\t * itself for an id form. A domain object's {@code toString()} can carry patient data, and this\n+\t * would write it to the log on every denial, where an identifier names the record without\n+\t * disclosing it.\n+\t */\n+\tprivate static void logDenial(DomainObjectAuthorizationRule rule, Class<?> type, Object permission, Object identifier) {\n+\t\tlog.debug(\"{} denied '{}' on {}{}\", rule.getClass().getName(), permission, type != null ? type.getName() : null,\n+\t\t    identifier != null ? \" with identifier \" + identifier : \"\");\n+\t}\n+\n+\t/**\n+\t * The identifier of a denied target, for the log line only, in the order of what it costs to read:\n+\t * the identifier a {@link HibernateProxy} already holds, read from its initializer so a lazy target\n+\t * is not loaded to label a log line - the same reason {@link #targetClassOf(Object)} reads the\n+\t * persistent class - then {@code getId()}, then the uuid, and nothing at all rather than throwing.\n+\t * <p>\n+\t * The uuid is the last resort because it is the read that can cost something: for the targets with\n+\t * no id to give it is the only identifier there is. {@code GlobalProperty}, {@code Role},\n+\t * {@code Privilege}, {@code DrugIngredient}, {@code FieldAnswer} and {@code AlertRecipient} have a\n+\t * natural key and throw {@link UnsupportedOperationException} from {@code getId()} by design, and\n+\t * an unsaved object has no id yet.\n+\t * <p>\n+\t * Each read is guarded on its own by {@link #readIdentifier(Supplier, Object)}, so one that fails\n+\t * falls through to the next and the last of them to nothing: a target that cannot answer costs the\n+\t * log line its identifier rather than the call its denial. This runs inside a predicate that must\n+\t * not throw, and the denial is the answer the caller is owed.\n+\t */\n+\tprivate static Object identifierOf(Object target) {\n+\t\tif (target instanceof HibernateProxy hibernateProxy) {\n+\t\t\tObject identifier = readIdentifier(() -> hibernateProxy.getHibernateLazyInitializer().getIdentifier(), target);\n+\t\t\tif (identifier != null) {\n+\t\t\t\treturn identifier;\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\n+\t\tif (!(target instanceof OpenmrsObject openmrsObject)) {\n+\t\t\treturn null;\n+\t\t}\n+\n+\t\tObject id = readIdentifier(openmrsObject::getId, target);\n+\t\treturn id != null ? id : readIdentifier(openmrsObject::getUuid, target);\n+\t}\n+\n+\t/**\n+\t * One guarded read of a denied target's identifier, so no step of {@link #identifierOf(Object)} can\n+\t * turn a denial into an exception - see there for why nothing may be thrown from here.\n+\t */\n+\tprivate static Object readIdentifier(Supplier<Object> identifier, Object target) {\n+\t\ttry {\n+\t\t\treturn identifier.get();\n+\t\t} catch (Exception e) {\n+\t\t\tlog.debug(\"Could not read an identifier of a denied {}; falling back to the next one\",\n+\t\t\t    targetClassOf(target).getName(), e);\n+\t\t\treturn null;\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\n+\t/**\n+\t * The entity class behind {@code target}, unwrapping a Hibernate or Spring AOP proxy so a rule\n+\t * registered for {@code Obs} still matches a lazily loaded or\n+\t * {@code @AuthorizeReturnObject}-proxied one. Reads the persistent class rather than the\n+\t * implementation: resolving the type must not initialize a lazy proxy, because this runs once per\n+\t * element of a {@code @PostFilter}ed collection.\n+\t */\n+\tprivate static Class<?> targetClassOf(Object target) {\n+\t\tif (target instanceof HibernateProxy hibernateProxy) {\n+\t\t\treturn hibernateProxy.getHibernateLazyInitializer().getPersistentClass();\n+\t\t}\n+\n+\t\treturn ClassUtils.getUserClass(AopProxyUtils.ultimateTargetClass(target));\n \t}\n \n-\tprivate boolean hasPermission(Object permission) {\n+\t/**\n+\t * The privilege half of the check, shared by both overloads: true if the current user holds\n+\t * {@code permission}, otherwise false with the name handed to {@link MissingPrivilegeRecorder} so a\n+\t * denial can report it (see the class javadoc). Resolved through {@link FilterPassPrivilegeCache}\n+\t * so a {@code @PostFilter} resolves its privilege once per pass rather than once per element;\n+\t * recording stays outside that cache, and is a no-op during a filter pass anyway.\n+\t */\n+\tprivate boolean holdsPrivilege(Object permission) {\n \t\tif (permission == null) {\n \t\t\treturn false;\n \t\t}\n \n \t\tString privilege = permission.toString();\n-\t\tif (Context.hasPrivilege(privilege)) {\n+\t\tif (FilterPassPrivilegeCache.holdsPrivilege(privilege, PrivilegeResolution::holdsPrivilege)) {\n \t\t\treturn true;\n \t\t}\n \n"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2fddec62-6800-4d87-a7fa-903e577e1b61","uuid":"b84c92a3-cdfd-4840-9fbd-836149dc116d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:05:25.401Z","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"--- a/api/src/main/java/org/openmrs/security/OpenmrsPermissionEvaluator.java\n+++ b/api/src/main/java/org/openmrs/security/OpenmrsPermissionEvaluator.java\n@@ -10,11 +10,26 @@\n package org.openmrs.security;\n \n import java.io.Serializable;\n+im